// `std:fs` — the filesystem, and VL's first FALLIBLE std module.
//
// Everything before this module was total: `std:fmt`, `std:array` and
// `std:buffer` either answer or trap, because a bad index is a BUG. A missing
// file is not a bug. It is a normal program state that the caller must be able
// to handle, and `docs/error-handling-design.md` (owner-ruled 2026-07-26) says
// how VL spells that: **errors are values, carried in the return type as a
// union**. There is no `throw` here, no `try`, and nothing to catch. `__trap__`
// appears in this file exactly zero times.
//
// THE THREE LAYERS, and which one each function uses:
//   - `T | null`    — expected absence, no reason to carry. Not used for I/O
//                     failure; used for "the operation succeeded and has
//                     nothing to report" (`writeFile` returns `IoResult`, which
//                     is `IoError | null`).
//   - `T | IoError` — reasoned failure. Every read/list/stat surface.
//   - trap          — a bug. Not reachable from anything in this module.
//
// `IoError = { code: i32, msg: string }` is the ruled I/O shape: `code` is the
// WASI errno (`ENOENT` = 44, and the constants below name the ones worth
// testing), `msg` is a human sentence naming the operation and the path. The
// design doc's FLOOR shape is `{ msg: string }` and `IoError` is meant to
// extend it; be warned that at this compiler head the extension does not
// interoperate the way the doc implies — a value of shape `{code, msg}` does
// NOT satisfy a `{msg}` parameter (structural width subtyping is a codegen
// reject), and `x is Err` over a union whose arm is `IoError` is a hard type
// error rather than a match. So a caller handles `IoError` by name, and a
// function that wants to accept "any error" must spell the union.
//
// THE LADDER, AND ITS SHORTHAND. The `as` trio is `as` propagates, `as?` coalesces
// to null, `as!` traps. The PROPAGATING form is BUILT — this module was the
// consumer its trigger named ("once std:fs / parsing chains prove the ladder
// boilerplate"), so writing this module is what fired it. Both spellings work:
//
//     function head(p: string): string | IoError {
//       const src = readTextFile(p)
//       if src is IoError { return src }      // re-raise, unchanged
//       return src.slice(0, 80)               // `src` is a string here
//     }
//
//     function head(p: string): string | IoError {
//       return (readTextFile(p) as string).slice(0, 80)   // the same function
//     }
//
// `x as T` names the arm the caller WANTS; everything else early-returns to the
// caller unchanged. The checker enforces that the propagated remainder fits the
// enclosing return type, so the second form is exactly as total as the first.
//
// `as?` and `as!` are RULED and NOT BUILT — they need their own surface syntax,
// where `as` needed none. Until then a miss you want as `null` or as a trap is
// the explicit check.
//
// The bodies below keep the explicit `is IoError { return e }` form deliberately:
// this module is the measurement of that boilerplate, and it also has to compile
// under a seed that predates the cast.
//
// NAMING, AND TWO DELIBERATE BREAKS WITH THE REST OF std. Both are required
// reading before "fixing" them, per `docs/internals/std-api-review.md`.
//
// (1) NOT `self`-FIRST, so these are free functions and not UFCS methods. `self`
// is not a style convention in VL — it is the UFCS SWITCH, matched by the
// compiler on the literal parameter name, so a first parameter named `path` makes
// method syntax unspellable. That is the point: the thing operated on here is the
// FILE, not the string naming it, and `"hello".readTextFile()` would type-check
// and be nonsense. Naming the parameter `path` removes that spelling by
// construction rather than by convention.
//
// (2) NOUN-FIRST for the two questions — `pathKind` and `pathExists`, not `kind`
// and `exists`. VL has no namespace import, so every export lands in the
// importer's flat top-level scope and a bare `exists` collides with every other
// existence check a program has. The prefix also marks the real split in this
// module: `readFile`/`writeFile`/`listDir` OPERATE ON a file, while these two ASK
// ABOUT a path. `pathKind` is deliberately not `stat`, because `stat` promises
// POSIX's `stat` — size, mtime, mode — and this returns only file-or-directory;
// a name that overpromises spends the good name before the feature arrives.
//
// WHAT CROSSES THE FLOOR. The intrinsics take and return `u8[]` — packed GC byte
// arrays, no linear memory, no pointers, no capacities, no size negotiation.
// Paths cross as UTF-8 bytes because that is what POSIX actually stores, and
// file CONTENT crosses as bytes because a file is bytes: a PNG, a `.wasm` and a
// zip are not text and must survive a round trip. Text is a DECODING on top,
// which is why `readFile`/`readTextFile` are two functions with two types and
// not one function with a flag. `std:utf8` owns the conversion and the
// invalid-input policy in both directions.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE DOES NOT DO. No path manipulation (join, normalize, extension
// — that is a future `std:fs/path`, and until it exists `dir + "/" + name` is
// the join). No open file handles, no seeking, no streaming, no partial reads:
// every read here is whole-file, so a file larger than memory is not readable
// through this surface. No metadata beyond file-or-directory — no size, no
// mtime, no permissions, because the floor does not carry them. No mkdir, no
// remove, no rename, no symlink inspection. Each of those is a floor intrinsic
// that does not exist yet, and adding a std wrapper for a syscall VL cannot make
// would be a name that fails at emit.
//
// CONCURRENCY AND TOCTOU. Nothing here is atomic against another process. A
// path that `pathExists` reported yesterday can be gone by the next line, which is
// why nothing in this module pre-checks on the caller's behalf: `readFile` does
// not stat first to produce a prettier error, it just reads and reports what the
// host said. A pre-check would cost a syscall and buy a race.

import { Utf8Error, decodeUtf8, decodeUtf8At, encodeUtf8 } from "std:utf8"
// `toString` is `std:fmt`'s export as of 2026-09-01, not the ambient builtin it was
// (owner ruling, DECISIONS.md). Two errno/offset renderings here need it. `std:utf8`
// above already pulls `std:fmt` in for the same reason, so this import adds a NAME and
// no bytes; the price and why a private renderer was refused are in `std:utf8`'s header.
import { toString } from "std:fmt"

// The blessed I/O error shape (`docs/error-handling-design.md` O3). `code` is
// the WASI errno as a POSITIVE number — the floor reports `-errno` and this
// module negates once, at the boundary, so a caller never sees a sign
// convention.
export type IoError = { code: i32, msg: string }

// The result of an operation that has NOTHING to report when it succeeds: a
// reason, or `null` for "it worked". This is the `T | null` layer of the ruled
// model doing exactly what it is for — a successful whole-file write has no
// value to hand back, and inventing one (a byte count the floor never reported,
// a boolean that is only ever `true`) would be fabricating information.
//
// Spelled as a NAMED alias rather than inline as `IoError | null` in each
// signature. That WAS a constraint and is now a preference: a struct appearing
// both in an inline `S | null` and in some other union of the same module used
// to fail at emit with `ref valtype with no interned shape`, because only the
// declaration route interned the shape. Both routes intern it now — the inline
// spelling lowers as the `nulvariant` niche. The alias stays because it names
// the concept once and reads better at every call site, not because the other
// spelling is unavailable.
export type IoResult = IoError | null

// What a path names. A literal union rather than a struct or an i32: it is one
// closed question with two answers today, `match` dispatches it exhaustively,
// and a third answer (`"symlink"`) is a one-word edit that makes every existing
// `match` fail loudly instead of silently falling through.
export type FileKind = "file" | "dir"

// The errno numbers worth naming, in WASI preview1's numbering (which is what
// the host reports and what `docs/error-handling-design.md` already uses when it
// writes `{ code: 44, msg: "no such file" }`). These are the codes a program
// actually branches on; the rest are rendered by number.
export const EACCES = 2
export const EILSEQ = 25
export const EINVAL = 28
export const EIO = 29
export const EISDIR = 31
export const ENOENT = 44
export const ENOSPC = 51
export const ENOTDIR = 54
export const EPERM = 63

// The errno as a short name, or `"errno N"` for everything else. Deliberately
// not a 76-arm table: these are the ones a caller can do something about, and a
// number is a perfectly good rendering of a code nobody branches on.
function errName(code: i32) {
  if code == EACCES { return "EACCES (permission denied)" }
  if code == EILSEQ { return "EILSEQ (invalid byte sequence)" }
  if code == EINVAL { return "EINVAL (invalid argument)" }
  if code == EIO { return "EIO (i/o error)" }
  if code == EISDIR { return "EISDIR (is a directory)" }
  if code == ENOENT { return "ENOENT (no such file or directory)" }
  if code == ENOSPC { return "ENOSPC (no space left on device)" }
  if code == ENOTDIR { return "ENOTDIR (not a directory)" }
  if code == EPERM { return "EPERM (operation not permitted)" }
  "errno " + toString(code)
}

// The one message shape, so every error out of this module reads the same and a
// grep for one of them finds all of them. The operation is named because the
// path alone does not say whether the read or the decode failed.
function failed(op: string, path: string, code: i32) {
  "fs." + op + " " + path + ": " + errName(code)
}

// The errno for a floor call that answered EMPTY.
//
// This is the one piece of GLOBAL STATE in the module's contract, and it is a
// DEVIATION knowingly taken: `std-api-review.md` §2 flags ambient state, and the
// rule there is that the header must name the constraint that forced it.
//
// The constraint was that neither in-band shape lowered — `u8[] | i32` was
// check-clean invalid wasm and `u8[] | null` declined — so the reason had no
// way to travel with the result and had to go out of band through
// `__fs_errno__()`. Read it IMMEDIATELY after the failed call and never
// otherwise.
//
// THAT CONSTRAINT HAS SINCE LIFTED (#1806 shipped `u8[]` into the union box's
// kind vocabulary, so `u8[] | i32` now lowers and runs), and this wrapper is why
// the swap is cheap: an in-band floor changes `emptyErrno` and its three callers
// rather than every caller of the module. It is deliberately NOT taken in the
// same change that introduces the module — swapping the floor means re-cutting
// the intrinsics and both hosts, and the protocol below is correct as it stands.
//
// RETURNS 0 WHEN THE LAST CALL DID NOT FAIL, and that zero is load-bearing: the
// floor reports failure by answering an EMPTY `u8[]`, which an empty FILE and an
// empty DIRECTORY also produce. The errno is what tells those apart, so a zero
// must reach `readOk` intact rather than being folded to `EIO` the way the
// i32-returning `__fs_write__`/`__fs_stat__` paths fold theirs — there, a call
// has already declared itself failed and a zero would be a contract violation;
// here a zero is the ordinary answer.
//
// A negative errno is folded rather than propagated, so a floor that reports
// `-ENOENT` and one that reports `ENOENT` are the same to every caller.
function lastErrno(): i32 {
  const e = __fs_errno__()
  if e < 0 { return 0 - e }
  e
}

// Whether an EMPTY result from a `u8[]`-returning intrinsic is a failure, and if
// so which. Returns 0 for "not a failure — the file or directory really is
// empty". One home for the empty-plus-errno protocol, so the three call sites
// cannot drift, and so the swap to an in-band `u8[] | i32` floor (now expressible
// — see #1806) is a change to this function rather than to every caller.
function emptyErrno(): i32 { lastErrno() }

// The message for an empty path, which every entry point rejects before it
// reaches the floor. This is the ONLY policy this module applies ahead of the
// syscall, and it earns its place twice: `""` names nothing on any host (POSIX
// `openat` answers ENOENT, other hosts vary), and answering identically
// everywhere is worth one comparison.
//
// Only the MESSAGE is factored out, not the error value, and that is a
// constraint rather than a preference: a struct produced by a CALL and flowing
// into a union position does not lower — `return someHelper()` from a function
// returning `T | IoError` fails at emit with `ref valtype with no interned
// shape`. Every `IoError` in this file is therefore an object LITERAL written at
// the site that returns it. Factoring them into a constructor is the obvious
// tidy-up and it does not compile.
function emptyPathMsg(op: string) { "fs." + op + ": the path is empty" }

// `readFile(path)` — the whole file as bytes, or why not.
//
// BYTES, not text: this is the function that reads a PNG. Whole-file only —
// there is no handle, no offset and no way to read part of one, so the result is
// as large as the file and a file that does not fit in memory is not readable
// here.
export function readFile(path: string): u8[] | IoError {
  if path.length == 0 { return { code: EINVAL, msg: emptyPathMsg("readFile") } }
  const r = __fs_read__(encodeUtf8(path))
  // EMPTY is the floor's failure signal AND a legitimate empty file, so the
  // errno is the disambiguator — see `emptyErrno`.
  if r.length == 0 {
    const e = emptyErrno()
    if e != 0 { return { code: e, msg: failed("readFile", path, e) } }
  }
  r
}

// `readTextFile(path)` — the whole file, decoded as UTF-8.
//
// Two failures, one type: the read can fail (any errno the host reports) and the
// DECODE can fail (`EILSEQ`, with the byte offset in the message). The second is
// the honest answer to "this file is not text" — see `std:utf8` for why the
// strict decode is the default and `decodeUtf8Lossy` is the opt-out. A caller
// who wants the lossy behaviour reads bytes and decodes them itself; that is two
// lines and it is not this function's decision to make.
export function readTextFile(path: string): string | IoError {
  // Guarded here as well as in `readFile`, so the message names the function the
  // caller actually called. Every OTHER error below is forwarded verbatim from
  // the call that produced it, and keeps that call's operation name — which is
  // where the failure really happened, and is more useful than a re-wrap.
  if path.length == 0 {
    return { code: EINVAL, msg: emptyPathMsg("readTextFile") }
  }
  const bytes = readFile(path)
  if bytes is IoError { return bytes }
  const text = decodeUtf8(bytes)
  if text is Utf8Error {
    return { code: EILSEQ, msg: "fs.readTextFile " + path + ": " + text.msg }
  }
  text
}

// `writeFile(path, data)` — replace the file's contents with `data`.
//
// Returns `IoResult` — `null` IS the success case, because a successful write
// has nothing to report; no partial-write count exists at this floor, so
// returning one would be a fabrication. The call site reads
// `if e != null { … }`.
//
// Whether the file is created, truncated or replaced atomically is the HOST's
// policy, not this module's, and it is documented where the intrinsic is.
export function writeFile(path: string, data: u8[]): IoResult {
  if path.length == 0 {
    return { code: EINVAL, msg: emptyPathMsg("writeFile") }
  }
  const rc = __fs_write__(encodeUtf8(path), data)
  if rc == 0 { return null }
  // Negative is `-errno`. A POSITIVE non-zero is a floor protocol violation —
  // reported as EIO rather than trapped, because a std module has no business
  // killing the instance over a host that answered out of contract.
  let code = EIO
  if rc < 0 { code = 0 - rc }
  { code: code, msg: failed("writeFile", path, code) }
}

// `writeTextFile(path, text)` — the same, encoding `text` as UTF-8 first.
// Encoding cannot fail (`std:utf8`'s header), so this adds no failure mode.
export function writeTextFile(path: string, text: string): IoResult {
  if path.length == 0 {
    return { code: EINVAL, msg: emptyPathMsg("writeTextFile") }
  }
  writeFile(path, encodeUtf8(text))
}

// `listDir(path)` — the entries of a directory, as NAMES.
//
// Names, not paths: that is what the syscall reports, it is what you print, and
// re-attaching the directory is one `+` (`dir + "/" + name`) which the caller
// can skip entirely when it is about to `chdir`-relative or match on the name.
// Returning joined paths would be a lossy convenience — you cannot recover the
// name from a path without a path parser, and `std:fs/path` does not exist yet.
// `"."` and `".."` are NOT included (the host filters them); the order is the
// host's and is not sorted.
//
// A single entry that is not valid UTF-8 fails the WHOLE call with `EILSEQ`.
// That is deliberate: the alternative is silently dropping a file that exists,
// which turns a listing into a wrong answer instead of a loud one. The message
// carries the byte offset within the block so the entry is identifiable.
export function listDir(path: string): string[] | IoError {
  if path.length == 0 { return { code: EINVAL, msg: emptyPathMsg("listDir") } }
  const block = __fs_list__(encodeUtf8(path))
  // An EMPTY block is a failure or an empty directory; the errno separates them.
  if block.length == 0 {
    const e = emptyErrno()
    if e != 0 { return { code: e, msg: failed("listDir", path, e) } }
  }
  const out: string[] = []
  let start = 0
  let i = 0
  // `i` runs one PAST the end so the final entry — which has no separator after
  // it — is emitted by the same block as every other. `atSep` is computed in two
  // steps rather than folded into one `||`, so nothing indexes the array at
  // `length`.
  while i <= block.length {
    let atSep = i == block.length
    if !atSep { atSep = block[i] == 0x00 }
    if atSep {
      // Empty runs are skipped rather than yielded, which makes this agnostic
      // to whether the host treats 0x00 as a SEPARATOR or a TERMINATOR — a
      // filename is never empty, so a zero-length run carries no information.
      if i > start {
        const name = decodeUtf8At(block, start, i - start)
        if name is Utf8Error {
          return {
            code: EILSEQ,
            msg: "fs.listDir " +
              path +
              ": entry at byte " +
              toString(start + name.at) +
              " is not UTF-8",
          }
        }
        out.push(name)
      }
      start = i + 1
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  out
}

// `pathKind(path)` — what `path` names, or why the question could not be answered.
//
// A missing path is an ERROR here (`ENOENT`), not a third `FileKind`: "there is
// nothing here" is not a kind of thing, and folding it into the success arm is
// what makes callers forget that `EACCES` is also possible. `pathExists` is the
// function that turns ENOENT into a boolean, and it is separate precisely so
// that decision is made once and visibly.
export function pathKind(path: string): FileKind | IoError {
  if path.length == 0 { return { code: EINVAL, msg: emptyPathMsg("pathKind") } }
  const k = __fs_stat__(encodeUtf8(path))
  if k == 0 { return "file" }
  if k == 1 { return "dir" }
  let code = EIO
  if k < 0 { code = 0 - k }
  { code: code, msg: failed("pathKind", path, code) }
}

// `pathExists(path)` — whether `path` names anything, or why that is unanswerable.
//
// `boolean | IoError`, NOT `boolean`, and this is the whole reason the function
// is worth writing down. "Does not exist" and "you are not allowed to look" are
// different facts, and a `boolean` return has to lie about one of them.
// `Path::exists()` in Rust and `os.path.exists` in Python both answer `false`
// for a permission error, and both ship a documented footgun where a program
// creates a file it was told was absent, or reports a config missing when the
// truth is the directory is unreadable.
//
// So exactly two errnos become `false`, and both mean "nothing is there":
//   - ENOENT  — no such file or directory;
//   - ENOTDIR — a component of the path is not a directory, so nothing can be
//               at the end of it.
// Everything else — EACCES, EPERM, EIO, a loop, a name too long — propagates
// with its reason intact.
export function pathExists(path: string): boolean | IoError {
  const k = pathKind(path)
  if k is IoError {
    if k.code == ENOENT { return false }
    if k.code == ENOTDIR { return false }
    return k
  }
  true
}
